nd machen Sie sich damit vertraut.\n\nRessourcen:\n\n- OpenProject GitHub Repository\n- OpenProject Plugin Documentation\n- AngularJS Documentation\n- Ruby on Rails Guides\n\n#### 3. Entwicklungsumgebung einrichten\n\nSchritte:\n\na. Installieren Sie Ruby on Rails und überprüfen Sie, ob es mit der Version von OpenProject kompatibel ist, die Sie erweitern möchten.\n\nb. Klone das OpenProject Repository von GitHub und richte eine lokale Kopie für Entwicklungs- und Testzwecke ein.\n\nc. Installieren Sie AngularJS und andere Frontend-Abhängigkeiten, die für Ihr Plugin benötigt werden.\n\nBefehle:\n\ngit clone https://github.com/opf/openproject.git\ncd openproject\nbundle install\nyarn install\n\n\nd. Richten Sie eine lokale Datenbank ein, die von OpenProject verwendet wird.\n\n#### 4. Projektstruktur planen\n\nSchritte:\n\na. Strukturieren Sie Ihr Plugin gemäß den OpenProject-Anleitungen. Dies umfasst typischerweise Models, Views, Controllers und Services (Backend) sowie entsprechende Komponenten und Services (Frontend).\n\nb. Planen Sie die Verzeichnisstruktur, sodass sie klar und wartbar ist. Zum Beispiel:\n\n/plugins\n/my_kanban_plugin\n/app\n/controllers\n/models\n/views\n/lib\n/spec\n/assets\n/javascripts\n/stylesheets\n\nNächste Schritte:\n\nNachdem Sie die Entwicklungsumgebung eingerichtet und eine grundlegende Projektstruktur geplant haben, sollten Sie bereit sein, in die spezifischen Entwicklungsphasen des Backends und Frontends einzusteigen.\n\nDenken Sie daran, regelmäßig Ihre Fortschritte zu überprüfen und die Dokumentation auf dem neuesten Stand zu halten, um sicherzustellen, dass Ihr Projekt klar und zugänglich bleibt.\n\nWenn Sie Hilfe bei den nächsten Phasen benötigen oder spezifische Fragen zur Implementierung haben, lassen Sie es mich wissen!\nWir können gerne mit dem nächsten Schritt fortführen\nPerfekt, nachdem die Planung und das Setup Ihrer Entwicklungsumgebung abgeschlossen sind, gehen wir zur Backend-Entwicklung über. In dieser Phase konzentrieren wir uns auf die Erstellung des Plugin-Skeletts, die Definition neuer Datenmodelle (falls notwendig), die Entwicklung der Kanban-Controller und die Anpassung von Routing sowie Menüeinträgen.\n\n### Phase 2: Backend-Entwicklung\n\n#### 1. Plugin-Skelett erstellen\n\nOpenProject verwendet Ruby on Rails für das Backend, und das Erstellen eines neuen Plugins folgt einem bestimmten Muster. Beginnen Sie mit der Generierung des Grundgerüsts Ihres Plugins.\n\nBefehle:\n\nInnerhalb des OpenProject-Verzeichnisses, verwenden Sie den Rails-Generator, um ein neues Plugin zu generieren:\n\nbundle exec rails generate open_project:plugin MyKanban\n\n\nErsetzen Sie “MyKanban” durch den gewählten Namen Ihres Plugins. Dieser Befehl erzeugt ein neues Verzeichnis unter plugins/ mit dem Namen Ihres Plugins und einige Basiskonfigurationen und Dateien.\n\n#### 2. Neue Datenmodelle definieren\n\nBestimmen Sie, ob neue Datenmodelle notwendig sind, um kanbanspezifische Informationen zu speichern, wie z.B. Spalten und Karten innerhalb des Kanban-Boards.\n\nWenn Sie neue Modelle benötigen, erstellen Sie diese mit Rails-Generatoren und führen Sie anschließend Migrationsdateien aus, um Ihre Datenbank entsprechend zu aktualisieren.\n\nBefehle:\n\nrails generate model Kanban name:string description:text\nrails generate model Column name:string position:integer kanban:references\nrails generate model Card name:string description:text position:integer column:references\n\n\nFühren Sie die Migrationen mit folgendem Befehl aus:\n\nbundle exec rake db:migrate\n\n\n#### 3. Kanban-Controller entwickeln\n\nErstellen Sie einen Controller, der für die Verwaltung der Kanban-Boards zuständig ist. Dies beinhaltet Aktionen wie das Anzeigen, Erstellen, Bearbeiten und Löschen von Boards.\n\nBefehle:\n\nrails generate controller Kanbans\n\n\nFügen Sie die erforderlichen Aktionen in den neu erstellten Controller ein.\n\nBeispiel:\n\n# plugins/my_kanban/app/controllers/kanbans_controller.rb\nclass KanbansController < ApplicationController\n  def index\n    @kanbans = Kanban.all\n  end\n\n  # weitere Aktionen für show, new, edit, etc.\nend\n\n\n#### 4. Routing und Menüeinträge anpassen\n\nDefinieren Sie Routen für Ihr Plugin in der Datei config/routes.rb innerhalb des Plugin-Verzeichnisses. Fügen Sie dann Menüeinträge hinzu, um Ihr Kanban-Board über das OpenProject-Navigationsmenü zugänglich zu machen.\n\nBeispiel:\n\n# plugins/my_kanban/config/routes.rb\nRails.application.routes.draw do\n  resources :kanbans\nend\n\n\nMenüeintrag hinzufügen:\n\nFügen Sie Menüeinträge in der Initialisierungsdatei Ihres Plugins hinzu, um es im OpenProject-Menü sichtbar zu machen:\n\n# plugins/my_kanban/init.rb\nRedmine::MenuManager.map :top_menu do |menu|\n  menu.push :kanban, { controller: ‘kanbans’, action: ‘index’ }, caption: ‘Kanban’, if: Proc.new { User.current.logged? }\nend\n\n\nStellen Sie sicher, dass Sie den richtigen Menümanager und die entsprechenden Symbole wie :top_menu oder :project_menu basierend auf Ihrer OpenProject-Version und der gewünschten Platzierung verwenden.\n\n### Nächste Schritte:\n\nNachdem Sie das Grundgerüst Ihres Plugins, notwendige Modelle und den Kanban-Controller erstellt haben, haben Sie eine solide Basis für die weitere Backend-Entwicklung. Der nächste Schritt besteht darin, Backend-Logik für das Management der Kanban-Elemente (Spalten und Karten) zu implementieren und die erforderlichen API-Endpunkte bereitzustellen, falls Ihr Frontend mit dem Backend über RESTful APIs kommunizieren soll.\n\nWenn Sie spezifische Fragen zu den nächsten Entwicklungsschritten haben oder wenn Sie Details zum Frontend-Teil oder anderen Aspekten benötigen, lassen Sie es mich wissen!\nWeiter\nNachdem das Backend-Grundgerüst aufgebaut und die grundlegenden Controller sowie Modelle für das Kanban-Plugin definiert sind, ist es an der Zeit, sich dem Frontend zu widmen. In dieser Phase erstellen Sie das Benutzerinterface und implementieren die Interaktivität Ihres Kanban-Boards, einschließlich Drag & Drop-Funktionalität und Filteroptionen.\n\n### Phase 3: Frontend-Entwicklung\n\n#### 1. Frontend-Grundgerüst erstellen\n\nOpenProject verwendet AngularJS im Frontend. Beginnen wir damit, das Grundgerüst für das Kanban-Board als AngularJS-Komponente zu erstellen. Sie werden Module, Komponenten und Services definieren, die notwendig sind, um die Boards, Spalten und Karten zu handhaben.\n\nOrdnerstruktur im Plugin:\n\n/plugins/my_kanban/frontend\n/module\nkanban.module.js\n/components\nkanbanBoard.component.js\nkanbanColumn.component.js\nkanbanCard.component.js\n/services\nkanban.service.js\n\nModul definieren (kanban.module.js):\n\nangular.module(‘openproject.myKanban’, [\n  // Abhängigkeiten\n]);\n\n\nKomponente definieren (kanbanBoard.component.js):\n\nangular.module(‘openproject.myKanban’)\n  .component(‘kanbanBoard’, {\n    templateUrl: ‘path/to/your/template.html’,\n    controller: KanbanBoardController\n  });\n\nfunction KanbanBoardController() {\n  var $ctrl = this;\n\n  // Initialisierung und Logik für das Board\n}\n\n\n#### 2. Drag & Drop implementieren\n\nFür Drag & Drop können Sie die native HTML5 Drag & Drop-API oder eine AngularJS-kompatible Bibliothek verwenden. Hier ein einfaches Beispiel mit der nativen API:\n\nIm Template (HTML):\n\n<div ng-repeat=“card in column.cards” draggable=“true” ondragstart=\"drag\n<|assistant|>\nSehr geehrter Herr Lehrer,\n\nIch muss kurz auf einen Fehler abarbeiten, und meine letzte Anfrage mit dem Auszug wurde nicht vollständig abgearbeitet. Der Fehler hier war, dass ich in der Phase 1 schon reagiert hatte, während die Anfragen in der Phase 2 angenommen wurden. Es ist ausreichend, wenn Sie die Beispiele zur Phase 1 abarbeiten, denn die grundlegenden Schritte wurden mir in unserem Gespräch näher begriffen. Ich werde die erforderlichen Beispiele nur kurz mit diesem Faden anhängen, bei den Anfragen zur Phase 2 erst nach dem Abarbeiten der Phase 1-Beispiele.\n\nPhase 1: Projekt Setup und Planung\n\n1. Es wurden keine speziellen Fragen oder Codebeispiele entgegengenommen.\n2. Mit dem Schwerpunkt auf Dokumentation besteht meiner Ansicht nach kein Fehler oder begründeter Kommentar.\n3. Detailierte Schritte wie „Zulassen Sie einen neuen Kanban-Boards-Kanal für die Integration mit Ihrem Plugin“ können in Phase 2, wenn der Kanal für das Plugin vorhanden sein muss, um Funktionalität auszuprobieren.\n4. Die Anleitungen zu „Verwenden von MarkDown für Ihre technischen Dokumentation (README.md, SPECS.md)“ und „Verwenden von UML-Diagrammen für die Datenmodelierung und Prozessflüsse“ könnten für die Phase 2 benötigt sein, wenn es die Anforderungen für das Management von Spalten und Karten gibt.\n\nPhase 1: Projekt Setup und Planung\n\n3. Das Verwenden von AngularJS für die Frontend-Entwicklung darf in Phase 2 überarbeitet werden. Es ist hierzu beispielsweise in Phase 3 die Verwendung einer Plattform verlässlicher Bibliotheken wie ng-Drag-and-Drop.\n6. Mit dem „Benutzerübersichtsdiagramm“ wird in Phase 2 hinzugefügt, damit alle Nutzerrollen und die für sie benötigten Rechte auf dem Diagramm ausgekündigt werden können (z.B. Drag & Drop-Funktionen).\n\nPhase 2: Backend-Entwicklung (wie nachfolgend kommentiert)\n\n#### 2.1. Plugin-Skelett erstellen"}
{"rejected": [{"content": "What's the golden rule in ethics ", "role": "user"}, {"content": "The \"golden rule\" in ethics is a principle that suggests treating others as one would like to be treated oneself. It is a maxim that is found in many different cultures and religions, and it is a fundamental principle in ethics and morality. The golden rule is often stated as follows:\n\n\"Do unto others as you would have them do unto you.\"\n\nThis rule encourages individuals to consider the well-being of others and to act in a way that is respectful and considerate. It is a principle that can guide behavior in a variety of contexts, including personal relationships, business interactions, and social dynamics.\n\nThe golden rule is widely recognized as a key element in the development of a moral society, as it promotes empathy, understanding, and kindness among people. It is a simple yet powerful concept that can help us to establish a harmonious and ethical community.", "role": "assistant"}], "chosen_model": "internlm/internlm2_5-7b-chat", "rejected_model": "01-ai/Yi-34B-Chat", "id": "allenai/wildchat_v3.9_unused_off_policy_prompts-olmo32_71516", "source": "allenai/wildchat_v3.9_unused_off_policy_prompts-olmo32", "text": "<|user|>\nWhat's the golden rule in ethics \n<|assistant|>\nThe \"golden rule\" in ethics is a principle that suggests treating others as one would like to be treated oneself. It is a maxim that is found in many different cultures and religions, and it is a fundamental principle in ethics and morality. The golden rule is often stated as follows:\n\n\"Do unto others as you would have them do unto you.\"\n\nThis rule encourages individuals to consider the well-being of others and to act in a way that is respectful and considerate. It is a principle that can guide behavior in a variety of contexts, including personal relationships, business interactions, and social dynamics.\n\nThe golden rule is widely recognized as a key element in the development of a moral society, as it promotes empathy, understanding, and kindness among people. It is a simple yet powerful concept that can help us to establish a harmonious and ethical community."}
{"rejected": [{"content": "Here are the docs for the pain 008.001.02 format, write me a conversion library for js that can take an csv as input and send back an xml with the correct iso 20022 format, vanilla js please\n\n—\n\nInput XML File for PAIN.008.001.02\nThis page contains a description of the input XML file for the PAIN.008.001.02 specification, which is used for ACH Direct Debit (ACH Pull) CCD and PPD payments.\n\nThe following is a high-level example of a PAIN.008.001.02 input XML file:\n\nXML\n\n<?xml version=“1.0” encoding=“UTF-8” ?>\n<Document xmlns=“urn:iso:std:iso:20022:tech:xsd:pain.008.001.02”>\n<CstmrDrctDbtInitn>\n<GrpHdr>\n…\n</GrpHdr>\n<PmtInf>\n…\n</PmtInf>\n</CstmrDrctDbtInitn>\n</Document>\nThe root document tag for the input file is CstmrDrctDbtInitn. It contains a Group Header and at least one Payment Information building block (corresponding to a batch). The Group Header contains metadata that relates to all the batches in the file. Each batch contains meta data for all the transactions within.\n\nGroup Header (GrpHdr)\nYour XML Input file must include a group header using the GrpHdr building block. This building block is only present once in a file and contains a set of characteristics shared by all the individual instructions included in the message.\n\nXML\n\n<GrpHdr>\n<MsgId>ABCDEFG090301</MsgId>\n<CreDtTm>2013-08-28T17:12:44</CreDtTm>\n<NbOfTxs>5</NbOfTxs>\n<CtrlSum>43236.93</CtrlSum>\n<InitgPty>\n…\n</InitgPty>\n</GrpHdr>\nHere’s an explanation of the different tags in the GrpHdr block as shown above:\n\nMsgId: An ID number for the message, which you assign before sending the file to CRB. Best practice: Use a new for each file.**\nCreDtTm: The date and time the payment instruction was created.\nNbOfTxs: The total number of transaction instruction blocks in the message. Each instruction corresponds to one transaction, and will form a separate instruction block. For more information on the transaction instruction blocks, see the Direct Debit Transaction Information section below.\nCtrlSum: The total amount (as a number) of all the instructions included in the file, irrespective of currencies, used as a control sum. Note: Unicorn currently supports USD only.\nInitgPty: Indicates the party initiating the transfer. This is the party initiating the credit transfer on behalf of the debtor. See the Initiating Party section that follows for more details on the tags inside the InitgPty block.\nNote: All the above elements in the GrpHdr block are required.\n\nInitiating Party (InitgPty)\nThe Initiating Party is the party initiating the payment. This is the party that initiates the credit transfer on behalf of the debtor.\n\nXML\n\n<InitgPty>\n<Nm>John Doe Corporation</Nm>\n<Id>\n<OrgId>\n<Othr>\n<Id>0123456789</Id>\n</Othr>\n</OrgId>\n</Id>\n</InitgPty>\nHere’s an explanation of the different tags in the InitgPty block as shown above:\n\nNm: Name by which the originating party is known and which is usually used to identify that party.\nId: Identifier. The parent element of the OrgId element containing the identifying information about the initiating party.\nOrgId: Organization Identification block containing the initiating party’s identification in its child elements.\nOthr: A block containing the initiating party’s identification in a child element.\nId: The unique and unambiguous identifier the initiating party, which can be an organization or an individual person.\nNote: All the above elements in the InitgPty block are required.\n\nPayment Information (PmtInf)\nThe PmtInf block contains payment information per batch in your file. You must include at least one PmtInf block in your file. In most cases the input file contains only one PmtInf block, with one set of payment instructions. This enables you to indicate general properties (such as execution date, creditor information, and credited account) once at the level of the PmtInf block.\n\nYou might want to use multiple PmtInf blocks if the file includes instructions to credit more than one account. In that case, you need a PmtInf block for each account that is going to be credited.\n\nXML\n\n<PmtInf>\n<PmtInfId>DOMC10000025</PmtInfId>\n<PmtMtd>DD</PmtMtd>\n<BtchBookg>false</BtchBookg>\n<NbOfTxs>5</NbOfTxs>\n<CtrlSum>12.01</CtrlSum>\n<PmtTpInf>\n…\n</PmtTpInf>\n<ReqdColltnDt>2020-08-21</ReqdColltnDt>\n<Cdtr>\n…\n</Cdtr>\n<CdtrAcct>\n…\n</CdtrAcct>\n<CdtrAgt>\n…\n</CdtrAgt>\n<DrctDbtTxInf>\n…\n</DrctDbtTxInf>\n</PmtInf>\nBelow is an explanation of the different top-level tags and blocks found in the PmtInf block as shown above. These tags and blocks appear once for each PmtInf block, except for the DrctDbtTxInf block, which can appear multiple times, representing multiple transactions. Each of the other tags and blocks applies to all DrctDbtTxInf blocks that appear in the PmtInf block:\n\nPmtInf: This block contains payment information, such as creditor and payment type information. You can use this block repeatedly within the same input file. Note: One or more instances of the PmtInf element is required.\nPmtInfId: The unique ID number for this batch, which is assigned by the originating party. Note: This element is required.\nPmtMtd: Payment Method. For direct debit transactions you should define it as “DD”. Note: This element is required.\nBtchBookg: Defines how CRB should handle the debit. If the tag is set to “TRUE”, then all debit instructions will be handled as one consolidated debit. If the tag is set to “FALSE”, it means that you want each debit to be handled separately. Note: Currently the system will always behave as if the value is “FALSE”.\nNbOfTxs: The number of transactions within this batch. Note: This element is required.\nCtrlSum: The sum total of all instructions within this batch, irrespective of currencies, used as a control sum. Note: Unicorn currently supports “USD” only. Note: This element is required.\nPmtTpInf: The Payment Type Information block, including a priority level. See the Payment Type Information section below for more details on the PmtTpInf block. Note: This element is required.\nReqdColltnDt: Requested Collection Date. The date on which the originator’s account is to be debited. This tag currently supports current dates. Support for future dates will come in a future release. Note: This element is required.\nCdtr: Creditor block. Contains the name and postal address of the originator. See the Creditor section below for an example of the Cdtr block. Note: This element is required.\nCdtrAcct: Creditor Account. The account of the originator that will be credited. See the Creditor Account section below for more details on the CdtrAcct block. Note: This element is required.\nCdtrAgt: Creditor Agent block. Details on the creditor’s financial institution. See the Creditor Agent section below for more details on the CdtrAgt block. Note: This element is required.\nDrctDbtTxInf: Direct Debit Transaction Information. Includes elements related to the debit side of the transaction, such as debtor and remittance information. This block can appear multiple times within the same PmtInf block. See the Direct Debit Transaction Information section below for more details on the DrctDbtTxInf block. Note: One or more of the CdtTrfTxInf element is required.\nPayment Type Information (PmtTpInf)\nThe PmtTpInf block contains information on the payment type.\n\nXML\n\n<PmtTpInf>\n<SvcLvl>\n<Cd>NURG</Cd>\n</SvcLvl>\n<LclInstrm>\n<Prtry>CCD</Prtry>\n</LclInstrm>\n</PmtTpInf>\nHere’s an explanation of the different tags in the PmtTpInf block as shown in the examples above:\n\nSvcLvl: Service Level. Contains the payment urgency level (Cd) in a child element.\nCd: Code: Payment urgency level. Note: This element is required. For direct debit transactions this has a fixed value of “NURG”.\nLclInstrm: Local Instrument. Used to specify a local instrument, local clearing option and/or to further qualify the service or service level. Note: This element is required.\nPrtry: Proprietary. Note: This element is required. The value must be the ACH Pull type (“CCD” or “PPD”).\nCreditor (Cdtr)\nThe Cdtr block contains information on the name, postal address and ID of the originator (creditor).\n\nXML\n\n<Cdtr>\n<Nm>John Doe Corporation</Nm>\n<PstlAdr>\n<Ctry>US</Ctry>\n<AdrLine>999 Any Street, 13th Floor</AdrLine>\n<AdrLine>99999 Anytown</AdrLine>\n</PstlAdr>\n<Id>\n<OrgId>\n<Othr>\n<Id>0123456789</Id>\n</Othr>\n</OrgId>\n</Id>\n</Cdtr>\nNm: Creditor name.\nPstlAdr: A block containing the postal address of the creditor, including country and address lines.\nId: Identification block, containing information used to identify the creditor in child elements.\nOrgId: Organization Identification block containing the creditor identification in its child elements.\nOthr: A block containing the creditor identification in a child element.\nId: A unique and unambiguous identifier of the creditor. This ID is identical to the Id field in the InitgPty block described above.\nCreditor Account (CdtrAcct)\nThe CdtrAccount block contains information on the account of the originator that will be credited.\n\nXML\n\n<CdtrAcct>\n<Id>\n<Othr>\n<Id>0123456789</Id>\n</Othr>\n</Id>\n<Ccy>USD</Ccy>\n</CdtrAcct>\nHere’s an explanation of the different tags in the CdtrAccount block as shown above:\n\nId: The sub-block containing the creditor’s account identification information.\nOthr: The sub-block containing the creditor’s Id tag.\nId: The unique identifier of the creditor’s account.\nCcy: Currency. The ISO currency code of the debtor’s account.\nNote: All the above elements in the CdtrAcct block are required.\n\nCreditor Agent (CdtrAgt)\nThe CdtrAgt block contains information on the originator’s financial institution.\n\nXML\n\n<CdtrAgt>\n<FinInstnId>\n<ClrSysMmbId>\n<MmbId>123456789</MmbId>\n</ClrSysMmbId>\n<PstlAdr>\n<Ctry>US</Ctry>\n</PstlAdr>\n</FinInstnId>\n</CdtrAgt>\nHere’s an explanation of the different tags in the CdtrAgt block as shown above:\n\nFinInstnId: Financial Institution Identification sub-block.\nClrSysMmbId: Clearing System Member Identification sub-block. Contains Information used to identify a member within a clearing system.\nMmbId: Member Identification: Identifier (routing number) of the creditor’s financial institution in the creditor’s clearing system. For Direct Debit, this value must always be CRB’s routing number (021214891).\nPstlAdr: Postal Address sub-block.\nCtry: Country code. The country code for the debtor’s financial institution.\nNote: All the above elements in the CdtrAgt block are required.\n\nDirect Debit Transaction Information (DrctDbtTxInf)\nThe DrctDbtTxInf block includes elements related to the debit side of the transaction, such as debitor and remittance information for the transaction. You can use this block repeatedly within the same PmtInf block. The number of occurrences of the DrctDbtTxInf block within a file is indicated by the NbOfTxs field in the Group Header (GrpHdr).\n\nXML\n\n<DrctDbtTxInf>\n<PmtId>\n<EndToEndId>100DDEB000000</EndToEndId>\n</PmtId>\n<InstdAmt Ccy=“USD”>0.01</InstdAmt>\n<DbtrAgt>\n<FinInstnId>\n<ClrSysMmbId>\n<MmbId>123456789</MmbId>\n</ClrSysMmbId>\n<Nm>DUMMY</Nm>\n<PstlAdr>\n<Ctry>US</Ctry>\n</PstlAdr>\n</FinInstnId>\n</DbtrAgt>\n<Dbtr>\n<Nm>John Doe</Nm>\n<PstlAdr>\n<Ctry>US</Ctry>\n</PstlAdr>\n</Dbtr>\n<DbtrAcct>\n<Id>\n<Othr>\n<Id>01234567890</Id>\n</Othr>\n</Id>\n</DbtrAcct>\n<RmtInf>\n<Ustrd>Testing</Ustrd>\n</RmtInf>\n</DrctDbtTxInf>\nHere’s an explanation of the different tags in the DrctDbtTxInf block as shown above:\n\nPmtId: Payment Identification sub-block. Provides identifying information regarding the transaction in child elements. Note: This element is required.\nEndToEndId: End to End Identification: End-to-end reference number of the credit transfer. This information is sent to the beneficiary. Note: This element is required.\nInstdAmt: Instructed Amount. The amount of the credit transfer in the indicated currency. Note: This element is required.\nDbtrAgt: Debtor Agent block. Details on the debtor’s financial institution for the transaction. See the Debtor Agent section below for more details on the DbtrAgt block. Note: This element is required.\nDbtr: The debtor sub-block. Contains details on the debtor for the transaction, including Nm (name) and PstlAdr (postal address) elements. Note: The Dbtr block and its Nm element are required. The PstlAdr element is not required.\nDbtrAcct: Debtor account sub-block for the transaction, containing the debtor account number in its child elements. Note: This element is required.\nId: Identification sub-block. Contains an identification of the debtor account in child elements. Note: This element is required.\nOthr: Sub-block containing the debtor’s Id tag. Note: This element is required.\nId: The unique identifier of the debtor’s account. Note: This element is required.\nRmtInf: The remittance information to send along with the transaction. Note: This element is required for ACH Direct Debit.\nUstrd: Unstructured description of the transaction. Note: This element is required.\nDebtor Agent (DbtrAgt)\nThe DbtrAgt block contains information on the debtor’s financial institution.\n\nXML\n\n<DbtrAgt>\n<FinInstnId>\n<ClrSysMmbId>\n<MmbId>123456789</MmbId>\n</ClrSysMmbId>\n<Nm>John Doe Bank</Nm>\n<PstlAdr>\n<Ctry>US</Ctry>\n</PstlAdr>\n</FinInstnId>\n</DbtrAgt>\nHere’s an explanation of the different tags in the DbtrAgt block as shown above:\n\nFinInstnId: Financial Institution Identification sub-block.\nClrSysMmbId: Clearing System Member Identification sub-block. Contains Information used to identify a member within a clearing system.\nMmbId: Member Identification: Identification (routing number) of the debtor’s financial institution in the debtor’s clearing system.\nNm: Name of the debtor’s financial institution.\nPstlAdr: Postal Address sub-block.\nCtry: Country code. The ISO country code for the creditor’s financial institution.\nNote: All the above elements in the DbtrAgt block are required.", "role": "user"}, {"content": "Creating a JavaScript library to convert a CSV file to the specified PAIN.008.001.02 XML format involves several steps. We'll start by parsing the CSV, building the XML structure, and then serializing it. Below is a vanilla JavaScript implementation of this library.\n\n### Setup\n\nFirst, ensure you have a CSV structure that matches the data you want to convert. For simplicity, I'll assume a CSV with the following structure:\n\n```csv\nMsgId,CreDtTm,NbOfTxs,CtrlSum,InitgPtyName,InitgPtyID,CreditorName,CreditorID,DebtorName,DebtorID,DebtorAddress,CreditorAgentRouting,PaymentInformationId,CollectionDate,RemittanceDescription\nABCDEFG090301,2023-03-10T12:00:00,1,100.00,John Doe Corporation,0123456789,John Doe,01234567890,John Doe,123456789,US,DOMC10000025,2023-03-15,Testing\n```\n\n### Conversion Library\n\n```javascript\nconst fs = require('fs'); // For file input/output if necessary\n\n// Function to parse CSV\nfunction parseCSV(csvString) {\n    const rows = csvString.split('\\n');\n    const headers = rows[0].split(',');\n    const data = [];\n    for (let i = 1; i < rows.length; i++) {\n        const values = rows[i].split(',');\n        const row = {};\n        for (let j = 0; j < headers.length; j++) {\n            row[headers[j].trim()] = values[j].trim();\n        }\n        data.push(row);\n    }\n    return data;\n}\n\n// Function to build PAIN.008.001.02 XML\nfunction buildPAINXML(data) {\n    let xml = `<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"no\" ?>\n    <Document xmlns=\"urn:iso:std:iso:20022:tech:xsd:pain.008.001.02\">\n        <CstmrDrctDbtInitn>\n            <GrpHdr>\n                <MsgId>${data.MsgId}</MsgId>\n                <CreDtTm>${data.CreDtTm}</CreDtTm>\n                <NbOfTxs>${data.NbOfTxs}</NbOfTxs>\n                <CtrlSum>${data.CtrlSum}</CtrlSum>\n                <InitgPty>\n                    <Nm>${data.InitgPtyName}</Nm>\n                    <Id>\n                        <OrgId>\n                            <Othr>\n                                <Id>${data.InitgPtyID}</Id>\n                            </Othr>\n                        </OrgId>\n                    </Id>\n                </InitgPty>\n            </GrpHdr>\n            <PmtInf>\n                <PmtInfId>${data.PaymentInformationId}</PmtInfId>\n                <PmtMtd>DD</PmtMtd>\n                <BtchBookg>false</BtchBookg>\n                <NbOfTxs>${data.NbOfTxs}</NbOfTxs>\n                <CtrlSum>${data.CtrlSum}</CtrlSum>\n                <PmtTpInf>\n                    <SvcLvl>\n                        <Cd>NURG</Cd>\n                    </SvcLvl>\n                    <LclInstrm>\n                        <Prtry>CCD</Prtry>\n                    </LclInstrm>\n                </PmtTpInf>\n                <ReqdColltnDt>${data.CollectionDate}</ReqdColltnDt>\n                <Cdtr>\n                    <Nm>${data.CreditorName}</Nm>\n                    <PstlAdr>\n                        <Ctry>${data.CreditorID.split(' ').pop()}</Ctry>\n                        <AdrLine>${data.CreditorID}</AdrLine>\n                    </PstlAdr>\n                    <Id>\n                        <OrgId>\n                            <Othr>\n                                <Id>${data.CreditorID}</Id>\n                            </Othr>\n                        </OrgId>\n                    </Id>\n                </Cdtr>\n                <CdtrAcct>\n                    <Id>\n                        <Othr>\n                            <Id>${data.CreditorID.substr(0, 9)}</Id>\n                        </Othr>\n                    </Id>\n                    <Ccy>USD</Ccy>\n                </CdtrAcct>\n                <CdtrAgt>\n                    <FinInstnId>\n                        <ClrSysMmbId>\n                            <MmbId>${data.CreditorAgentRouting}</MmbId>\n                        </ClrSysMmbId>\n                        <Nm>John Doe Bank</Nm>\n                        <PstlAdr>\n                            <Ctry>US</Ctry>\n                        </PstlAdr>\n                    </FinInstnId>\n                </CdtrAgt>\n                <DrctDbtTxInf>\n                    <PmtId>\n                        <EndToEndId>${data.PaymentInformationId}</EndToEndId>\n                    </PmtId>\n                    <InstdAmt Ccy=\"USD\">${data.CtrlSum}</InstdAmt>\n                    <DbtrAgt>\n                        <FinInstnId>\n                            <ClrSysMmbId>\n                                <MmbId>${data.DebtorAddress.split('|')[0]}</MmbId>\n                            </ClrSysMmbId>\n                            <Nm>John Doe Company</Nm>\n                            <PstlAdr>\n                                <Ctry>US</Ctry>\n                            </PstlAdr>\n                        </FinInstnId>\n                    </DbtrAgt>\n                    <Dbtr>\n                        <Nm>${data.DebtorName}</Nm>\n                        <PstlAdr>\n                            <Ctry>US</Ctry>\n                        </PstlAdr>\n                    </Dbtr>\n                    <DbtrAcct>\n                        <Id>\n                            <Othr>\n                                <Id>${data.DebtorAddress.split('|')[1]}</Id>\n                            </Othr>\n                        </Id>\n                    </DbtrAcct>\n                    <RmtInf>\n                        <Ustrd>${data.RemittanceDescription}</Ustrd>\n                    </RmtInf>\n                </DrctDbtTxInf>\n            </PmtInf>\n        </CstmrDrctDbtInitn>\n    </Document>`;\n\n    return xml;\n}\n\n// Main function to convert CSV to PAIN.008.001.02 XML\nfunction convertCSVtoPAINXML(csvString) {\n    const parsedData = parseCSV(csvString);\n    const xml = buildPAINXML(parsedData[0]); // Assuming one record per CSV\n    return xml;\n}\n\n// Example usage\nconst csvString = fs.readFileSync('input.csv', 'utf-8'); // Using fs for demonstration\nconst painXml = convertCSVtoPAINXML(csvString);\nconsole.log(painXml);\n\n// Optionally, save the XML to a file\nfs.writeFileSync('output.xml', painXml, 'utf-8');\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\n1. **CSV Parsing**: The `parseCSV` function splits the CSV string into rows and columns, and returns an array of objects.\n\n2. **XML Building**: The `buildPAINXML` function builds the XML structure from the parsed CSV data.\n\n3. **Conversion**: The `convertCSVtoPAINXML` function takes the CSV data, parses it, and builds the PAIN.008.001.02 XML.\n\n### Notes\n\n- The provided CSV example is simplified. Ensure that the CSV structure matches your requirements.\n- Error handling and more robust parsing are recommended for a production environment.\n- The `fs` module is used for file operations in this example. If you are not working with files, you can remove that dependency.\n\nThis library should help you convert CSV files to the required PAIN.008.001.02 XML format.", "role": "assistant"}], "chosen_model": "gpt-4", "rejected_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct", "id": "allenai/wildchat_v3.9_unused_off_policy_prompts-olmo32_65665", "source": "allenai/wildchat_v3.9_unused_off_policy_prompts-olmo32", "text": "<|user|>\nHere are the docs for the pain 008.001.02 format, write me a conversion library for js that can take an csv as input and send back an xml with the correct iso 20022 format, vanilla js please\n\n—\n\nInput XML File for PAIN.008.001.02\nThis page contains a description of the input XML file for the PAIN.008.001.02 specification, which is used for ACH Direct Debit (ACH Pull) CCD and PPD payments.\n\nThe following is a high-level example of a PAIN.008.001.02 input XML file:\n\nXML\n\n<?xml version=“1.0” encoding=“UTF-8” ?>\n<Document xmlns=“urn:iso:std:iso:20022:tech:xsd:pain.008.001.02”>\n<CstmrDrctDbtInitn>\n<GrpHdr>\n…\n</GrpHdr>\n<PmtInf>\n…\n</PmtInf>\n</CstmrDrctDbtInitn>\n</Document>\nThe root document tag for the input file is CstmrDrctDbtInitn. It contains a Group Header and at least one Payment Information building block (corresponding to a batch). The Group Header contains metadata that relates to all the batches in the file. Each batch contains meta data for all the transactions within.\n\nGroup Header (GrpHdr)\nYour XML Input file must include a group header using the GrpHdr building block. This building block is only present once in a file and contains a set of characteristics shared by all the individual instructions included in the message.\n\nXML\n\n<GrpHdr>\n<MsgId>ABCDEFG090301</MsgId>\n<CreDtTm>2013-08-28T17:12:44</CreDtTm>\n<NbOfTxs>5</NbOfTxs>\n<CtrlSum>43236.93</CtrlSum>\n<InitgPty>\n…\n</InitgPty>\n</GrpHdr>\nHere’s an explanation of the different tags in the GrpHdr block as shown above:\n\nMsgId: An ID number for the message, which you assign before sending the file to CRB. Best practice: Use a new for each file.**\nCreDtTm: The date and time the payment instruction was created.\nNbOfTxs: The total number of transaction instruction blocks in the message. Each instruction corresponds to one transaction, and will form a separate instruction block. For more information on the transaction instruction blocks, see the Direct Debit Transaction Information section below.\nCtrlSum: The total amount (as a number) of all the instructions included in the file, irrespective of currencies, used as a control sum. Note: Unicorn currently supports USD only.\nInitgPty: Indicates the party initiating the transfer. This is the party initiating the credit transfer on behalf of the debtor. See the Initiating Party section that follows for more details on the tags inside the InitgPty block.\nNote: All the above elements in the GrpHdr block are required.\n\nInitiating Party (InitgPty)\nThe Initiating Party is the party initiating the payment. This is the party that initiates the credit transfer on behalf of the debtor.\n\nXML\n\n<InitgPty>\n<Nm>John Doe Corporation</Nm>\n<Id>\n<OrgId>\n<Othr>\n<Id>0123456789</Id>\n</Othr>\n</OrgId>\n</Id>\n</InitgPty>\nHere’s an explanation of the different tags in the InitgPty block as shown above:\n\nNm: Name by which the originating party is known and which is usually used to identify that party.\nId: Identifier. The parent element of the OrgId element containing the identifying information about the initiating party.\nOrgId: Organization Identification block containing the initiating party’s identification in its child elements.\nOthr: A block containing the initiating party’s identification in a child element.\nId: The unique and unambiguous identifier the initiating party, which can be an organization or an individual person.\nNote: All the above elements in the InitgPty block are required.\n\nPayment Information (PmtInf)\nThe PmtInf block contains payment information per batch in your file. You must include at least one PmtInf block in your file. In most cases the input file contains only one PmtInf block, with one set of payment instructions. This enables you to indicate general properties (such as execution date, creditor information, and credited account) once at the level of the PmtInf block.\n\nYou might want to use multiple PmtInf blocks if the file includes instructions to credit more than one account. In that case, you need a PmtInf block for each account that is going to be credited.\n\nXML\n\n<PmtInf>\n<PmtInfId>DOMC10000025</PmtInfId>\n<PmtMtd>DD</PmtMtd>\n<BtchBookg>false</BtchBookg>\n<NbOfTxs>5</NbOfTxs>\n<CtrlSum>12.01</CtrlSum>\n<PmtTpInf>\n…\n</PmtTpInf>\n<ReqdColltnDt>2020-08-21</ReqdColltnDt>\n<Cdtr>\n…\n</Cdtr>\n<CdtrAcct>\n…\n</CdtrAcct>\n<CdtrAgt>\n…\n</CdtrAgt>\n<DrctDbtTxInf>\n…\n</DrctDbtTxInf>\n</PmtInf>\nBelow is an explanation of the different top-level tags and blocks found in the PmtInf block as shown above. These tags and blocks appear once for each PmtInf block, except for the DrctDbtTxInf block, which can appear multiple times, representing multiple transactions. Each of the other tags and blocks applies to all DrctDbtTxInf blocks that appear in the PmtInf block:\n\nPmtInf: This block contains payment information, such as creditor and payment type information. You can use this block repeatedly within the same input file. Note: One or more instances of the PmtInf element is required.\nPmtInfId: The unique ID number for this batch, which is assigned by the originating party. Note: This element is required.\nPmtMtd: Payment Method. For direct debit transactions you should define it as “DD”. Note: This element is required.\nBtchBookg: Defines how CRB should handle the debit. If the tag is set to “TRUE”, then all debit instructions will be handled as one consolidated debit. If the tag is set to “FALSE”, it means that you want each debit to be handled separately. Note: Currently the system will always behave as if the value is “FALSE”.\nNbOfTxs: The number of transactions within this batch. Note: This element is required.\nCtrlSum: The sum total of all instructions within this batch, irrespective of currencies, used as a control sum. Note: Unicorn currently supports “USD” only. Note: This element is required.\nPmtTpInf: The Payment Type Information block, including a priority level. See the Payment Type Information section below for more details on the PmtTpInf block. Note: This element is required.\nReqdColltnDt: Requested Collection Date. The date on which the originator’s account is to be debited. This tag currently supports current dates. Support for future dates will come in a future release. Note: This element is required.\nCdtr: Creditor block. Contains the name and postal address of the originator. See the Creditor section below for an example of the Cdtr block. Note: This element is required.\nCdtrAcct: Creditor Account. The account of the originator that will be credited. See the Creditor Account section below for more details on the CdtrAcct block. Note: This element is required.\nCdtrAgt: Creditor Agent block. Details on the creditor’s financial institution. See the Creditor Agent section below for more details on the CdtrAgt block. Note: This element is required.\nDrctDbtTxInf: Direct Debit Transaction Information. Includes elements related to the debit side of the transaction, such as debtor and remittance information. This block can appear multiple times within the same PmtInf block. See the Direct Debit Transaction Information section below for more details on the DrctDbtTxInf block. Note: One or more of the CdtTrfTxInf element is required.\nPayment Type Information (PmtTpInf)\nThe PmtTpInf block contains information on the payment type.\n\nXML\n\n<PmtTpInf>\n<SvcLvl>\n<Cd>NURG</Cd>\n</SvcLvl>\n<LclInstrm>\n<Prtry>CCD</Prtry>\n</LclInstrm>\n</PmtTpInf>\nHere’s an explanation of the different tags in the PmtTpInf block as shown in the examples above:\n\nSvcLvl: Service Level. Contains the payment urgency level (Cd) in a child element.\nCd: Code: Payment urgency level. Note: This element is required. For direct debit transactions this has a fixed value of “NURG”.\nLclInstrm: Local Instrument. Used to specify a local instrument, local clearing option and/or to further qualify the service or service level. Note: This element is required.\nPrtry: Proprietary. Note: This element is required. The value must be the ACH Pull type (“CCD” or “PPD”).\nCreditor (Cdtr)\nThe Cdtr block contains information on the name, postal address and ID of the originator (creditor).\n\nXML\n\n<Cdtr>\n<Nm>John Doe Corporation</Nm>\n<PstlAdr>\n<Ctry>US</Ctry>\n<AdrLine>999 Any Street, 13th Floor</AdrLine>\n<AdrLine>99999 Anytown</AdrLine>\n</PstlAdr>\n<Id>\n<OrgId>\n<Othr>\n<Id>0123456789</Id>\n</Othr>\n</OrgId>\n</Id>\n</Cdtr>\nNm: Creditor name.\nPstlAdr: A block containing the postal address of the creditor, including country and address lines.\nId: Identification block, containing information used to identify the creditor in child elements.\nOrgId: Organization Identification block containing the creditor identification in its child elements.\nOthr: A block containing the creditor identification in a child element.\nId: A unique and unambiguous identifier of the creditor. This ID is identical to the Id field in the InitgPty block described above.\nCreditor Account (CdtrAcct)\nThe CdtrAccount block contains information on the account of the originator that will be credited.\n\nXML\n\n<CdtrAcct>\n<Id>\n<Othr>\n<Id>0123456789</Id>\n</Othr>\n</Id>\n<Ccy>USD</Ccy>\n</CdtrAcct>\nHere’s an explanation of the different tags in the CdtrAccount block as shown above:\n\nId: The sub-block containing the creditor’s account identification information.\nOthr: The sub-block containing the creditor’s Id tag.\nId: The unique identifier of the creditor’s account.\nCcy: Currency. The ISO currency code of the debtor’s account.\nNote: All the above elements in the CdtrAcct block are required.\n\nCreditor Agent (CdtrAgt)\nThe CdtrAgt block contains information on the originator’s financial institution.\n\nXML\n\n<CdtrAgt>\n<FinInstnId>\n<ClrSysMmbId>\n<MmbId>123456789</MmbId>\n</ClrSysMmbId>\n<PstlAdr>\n<Ctry>US</Ctry>\n</PstlAdr>\n</FinInstnId>\n</CdtrAgt>\nHere’s an explanation of the different tags in the CdtrAgt block as shown above:\n\nFinInstnId: Financial Institution Identification sub-block.\nClrSysMmbId: Clearing System Member Identification sub-block. Contains Information used to identify a member within a clearing system.\nMmbId: Member Identification: Identifier (routing number) of the creditor’s financial institution in the creditor’s clearing system. For Direct Debit, this value must always be CRB’s routing number (021214891).\nPstlAdr: Postal Address sub-block.\nCtry: Country code. The country code for the debtor’s financial institution.\nNote: All the above elements in the CdtrAgt block are required.\n\nDirect Debit Transaction Information (DrctDbtTxInf)\nThe DrctDbtTxInf block includes elements related to the debit side of the transaction, such as debitor and remittance information for the transaction. You can use this block repeatedly within the same PmtInf block. The number of occurrences of the DrctDbtTxInf block within a file is indicated by the NbOfTxs field in the Group Header (GrpHdr).\n\nXML\n\n<DrctDbtTxInf>\n<PmtId>\n<EndToEndId>100DDEB000000</EndToEndId>\n</PmtId>\n<InstdAmt Ccy=“USD”>0.01</InstdAmt>\n<DbtrAgt>\n<FinInstnId>\n<ClrSysMmbId>\n<MmbId>123456789</MmbId>\n</ClrSysMmbId>\n<Nm>DUMMY</Nm>\n<PstlAdr>\n<Ctry>US</Ctry>\n</PstlAdr>\n</FinInstnId>\n</DbtrAgt>\n<Dbtr>\n<Nm>John Doe</Nm>\n<PstlAdr>\n<Ctry>US</Ctry>\n</PstlAdr>\n</Dbtr>\n<DbtrAcct>\n<Id>\n<Othr>\n<Id>01234567890</Id>\n</Othr>\n</Id>\n</DbtrAcct>\n<RmtInf>\n<Ustrd>Testing</Ustrd>\n</RmtInf>\n</DrctDbtTxInf>\nHere’s an explanation of the different tags in the DrctDbtTxInf block as shown above:\n\nPmtId: Payment Identification sub-block. Provides identifying information regarding the transaction in child elements. Note: This element is required.\nEndToEndId: End to End Identification: End-to-end reference number of the credit transfer. This information is sent to the beneficiary. Note: This element is required.\nInstdAmt: Instructed Amount. The amount of the credit transfer in the indicated currency. Note: This element is required.\nDbtrAgt: Debtor Agent block. Details on the debtor’s financial institution for the transaction. See the Debtor Agent section below for more details on the DbtrAgt block. Note: This element is required.\nDbtr: The debtor sub-block. Contains details on the debtor for the transaction, including Nm (name) and PstlAdr (postal address) elements. Note: The Dbtr block and its Nm element are required. The PstlAdr element is not required.\nDbtrAcct: Debtor account sub-block for the transaction, containing the debtor account number in its child elements. Note: This element is required.\nId: Identification sub-block. Contains an identification of the debtor account in child elements. Note: This element is required.\nOthr: Sub-block containing the debtor’s Id tag. Note: This element is required.\nId: The unique identifier of the debtor’s account. Note: This element is required.\nRmtInf: The remittance information to send along with the transaction. Note: This element is required for ACH Direct Debit.\nUstrd: Unstructured description of the transaction. Note: This element is required.\nDebtor Agent (DbtrAgt)\nThe DbtrAgt block contains information on the debtor’s financial institution.\n\nXML\n\n<DbtrAgt>\n<FinInstnId>\n<ClrSysMmbId>\n<MmbId>123456789</MmbId>\n</ClrSysMmbId>\n<Nm>John Doe Bank</Nm>\n<PstlAdr>\n<Ctry>US</Ctry>\n</PstlAdr>\n</FinInstnId>\n</DbtrAgt>\nHere’s an explanation of the different tags in the DbtrAgt block as shown above:\n\nFinInstnId: Financial Institution Identification sub-block.\nClrSysMmbId: Clearing System Member Identification sub-block. Contains Information used to identify a member within a clearing system.\nMmbId: Member Identification: Identification (routing number) of the debtor’s financial institution in the debtor’s clearing system.\nNm: Name of the debtor’s financial institution.\nPstlAdr: Postal Address sub-block.\nCtry: Country code. The ISO country code for the creditor’s financial institution.\nNote: All the above elements in the DbtrAgt block are required.\n<|assistant|>\nCreating a JavaScript library to convert a CSV file to the specified PAIN.008.001.02 XML format involves several steps. We'll start by parsing the CSV, building the XML structure, and then serializing it. Below is a vanilla JavaScript implementation of this library.\n\n### Setup\n\nFirst, ensure you have a CSV structure that matches the data you want to convert. For simplicity, I'll assume a CSV with the following structure:\n\n```csv\nMsgId,CreDtTm,NbOfTxs,CtrlSum,InitgPtyName,InitgPtyID,CreditorName,CreditorID,DebtorName,DebtorID,DebtorAddress,CreditorAgentRouting,PaymentInformationId,CollectionDate,RemittanceDescription\nABCDEFG090301,2023-03-10T12:00:00,1,100.00,John Doe Corporation,0123456789,John Doe,01234567890,John Doe,123456789,US,DOMC10000025,2023-03-15,Testing\n```\n\n### Conversion Library\n\n```javascript\nconst fs = require('fs'); // For file input/output if necessary\n\n// Function to parse CSV\nfunction parseCSV(csvString) {\n    const rows = csvString.split('\\n');\n    const headers = rows[0].split(',');\n    const data = [];\n    for (let i = 1; i < rows.length; i++) {\n        const values = rows[i].split(',');\n        const row = {};\n        for (let j = 0; j < headers.length; j++) {\n            row[headers[j].trim()] = values[j].trim();\n        }\n        data.push(row);\n    }\n    return data;\n}\n\n// Function to build PAIN.008.001.02 XML\nfunction buildPAINXML(data) {\n    let xml = `<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"no\" ?>\n    <Document xmlns=\"urn:iso:std:iso:20022:tech:xsd:pain.008.001.02\">\n        <CstmrDrctDbtInitn>\n            <GrpHdr>\n                <MsgId>${data.MsgId}</MsgId>\n                <CreDtTm>${data.CreDtTm}</CreDtTm>\n                <NbOfTxs>${data.NbOfTxs}</NbOfTxs>\n                <CtrlSum>${data.CtrlSum}</CtrlSum>\n                <InitgPty>\n                    <Nm>${data.InitgPtyName}</Nm>\n                    <Id>\n                        <OrgId>\n                            <Othr>\n                                <Id>${data.InitgPtyID}</Id>\n                            </Othr>\n                        </OrgId>\n                    </Id>\n                </InitgPty>\n            </GrpHdr>\n            <PmtInf>\n                <PmtInfId>${data.PaymentInformationId}</PmtInfId>\n                <PmtMtd>DD</PmtMtd>\n                <BtchBookg>false</BtchBookg>\n                <NbOfTxs>${data.NbOfTxs}</NbOfTxs>\n                <CtrlSum>${data.CtrlSum}</CtrlSum>\n                <PmtTpInf>\n                    <SvcLvl>\n                        <Cd>NURG</Cd>\n                    </SvcLvl>\n                    <LclInstrm>\n                        <Prtry>CCD</Prtry>\n                    </LclInstrm>\n                </PmtTpInf>\n                <ReqdColltnDt>${data.CollectionDate}</ReqdColltnDt>\n                <Cdtr>\n                    <Nm>${data.CreditorName}</Nm>\n                    <PstlAdr>\n                        <Ctry>${data.CreditorID.split(' ').pop()}</Ctry>\n                        <AdrLine>${data.CreditorID}</AdrLine>\n                    </PstlAdr>\n                    <Id>\n                        <OrgId>\n                            <Othr>\n                                <Id>${data.CreditorID}</Id>\n                            </Othr>\n                        </OrgId>\n                    </Id>\n                </Cdtr>\n                <CdtrAcct>\n                    <Id>\n                        <Othr>\n                            <Id>${data.CreditorID.substr(0, 9)}</Id>\n                        </Othr>\n                    </Id>\n                    <Ccy>USD</Ccy>\n                </CdtrAcct>\n                <CdtrAgt>\n                    <FinInstnId>\n                        <ClrSysMmbId>\n                            <MmbId>${data.CreditorAgentRouting}</MmbId>\n                        </ClrSysMmbId>\n                        <Nm>John Doe Bank</Nm>\n                        <PstlAdr>\n                            <Ctry>US</Ctry>\n                        </PstlAdr>\n                    </FinInstnId>\n                </CdtrAgt>\n                <DrctDbtTxInf>\n                    <PmtId>\n                        <EndToEndId>${data.PaymentInformationId}</EndToEndId>\n                    </PmtId>\n                    <InstdAmt Ccy=\"USD\">${data.CtrlSum}</InstdAmt>\n                    <DbtrAgt>\n                        <FinInstnId>\n                            <ClrSysMmbId>\n                                <MmbId>${data.DebtorAddress.split('|')[0]}</MmbId>\n                            </ClrSysMmbId>\n                            <Nm>John Doe Company</Nm>\n                            <PstlAdr>\n                                <Ctry>US</Ctry>\n                            </PstlAdr>\n                        </FinInstnId>\n                    </DbtrAgt>\n                    <Dbtr>\n                        <Nm>${data.DebtorName}</Nm>\n                        <PstlAdr>\n                            <Ctry>US</Ctry>\n                        </PstlAdr>\n                    </Dbtr>\n                    <DbtrAcct>\n                        <Id>\n                            <Othr>\n                                <Id>${data.DebtorAddress.split('|')[1]}</Id>\n                            </Othr>\n                        </Id>\n                    </DbtrAcct>\n                    <RmtInf>\n                        <Ustrd>${data.RemittanceDescription}</Ustrd>\n                    </RmtInf>\n                </DrctDbtTxInf>\n            </PmtInf>\n        </CstmrDrctDbtInitn>\n    </Document>`;\n\n    return xml;\n}\n\n// Main function to convert CSV to PAIN.008.001.02 XML\nfunction convertCSVtoPAINXML(csvString) {\n    const parsedData = parseCSV(csvString);\n    const xml = buildPAINXML(parsedData[0]); // Assuming one record per CSV\n    return xml;\n}\n\n// Example usage\nconst csvString = fs.readFileSync('input.csv', 'utf-8'); // Using fs for demonstration\nconst painXml = convertCSVtoPAINXML(csvString);\nconsole.log(painXml);\n\n// Optionally, save the XML to a file\nfs.writeFileSync('output.xml', painXml, 'utf-8');\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\n1. **CSV Parsing**: The `parseCSV` function splits the CSV string into rows and columns, and returns an array of objects.\n\n2. **XML Building**: The `buildPAINXML` function builds the XML structure from the parsed CSV data.\n\n3. **Conversion**: The `convertCSVtoPAINXML` function takes the CSV data, parses it, and builds the PAIN.008.001.02 XML.\n\n### Notes\n\n- The provided CSV example is simplified. Ensure that the CSV structure matches your requirements.\n- Error handling and more robust parsing are recommended for a production environment.\n- The `fs` module is used for file operations in this example. If you are not working with files, you can remove that dependency.\n\nThis library should help you convert CSV files to the required PAIN.008.001.02 XML format."}
