{"rejected": [{"content": "Consider a pipe with a length of 37.5 cm. If the temperature of the air is 12.5 °C and the pipe is closed at one end and open in the other, what is the frequency of the third harmonic (also called the 2nd overtone) for the pipe?\n\nPost your answer in hertz (Hz) and with 3 significant figures.", "role": "user"}, {"content": "To find the frequency of the third harmonic (the 2nd overtone) for the pipe, we need to first make sure the pipe is open at both ends and the air in the tube is still. Then we need to consider the frequency of the first harmonic, the fundamental frequency, as the starting point.\n\nThe fundamental frequency is determined by the length of the pipe and the density of the air. As the properties of air at 12.5 °C are given in the problem, we can find the speed of sound in air:\n\nCp = 1215.1 Pa·s·m^-1\n\nT = 12.5 degC = 125.05K = 125.05(73.14/100) ≈ 92.65 K\n\nv = sqrt(Cp/T) = sqrt(1215.1/92.65) ≈ 328.17 m/s\n\nThe length of the pipe is 37.5 cm and velocity of sound is 328.17 m/s.\n\nFor calculating frequency of third harmonic it is important to consider the size of the pipe, The formula for calculating fundamental frequency is:\n\n1/T = 1/L + 1/T1/2\n\nWhere T is the period of the fundamental frequency, T1/2 is the diameter of the wave, L is the length of the pipe, the frequency of the third harmonic will be 3* 1/T\n\nSo,\n\n1/T = 1/37.5 + 1/T1/2\n\n1/T = 1.7888 x 10^-12 s\n\nT = 1/1.7888 x 10^-12 s = 5551.5 Hz\n\nFrequency of the Third harmonic = 3 * 1/T = 3* 5551.5 = 16655 Hz\n\nSo the answer is 16655 Hz", "role": "assistant"}], "chosen_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct", "rejected_model": "allenai/tulu-2-7b", "id": "allenai/wildchat_v3.9_unused_off_policy_prompts-olmo32_37537", "source": "allenai/wildchat_v3.9_unused_off_policy_prompts-olmo32", "text": "<|user|>\nConsider a pipe with a length of 37.5 cm. If the temperature of the air is 12.5 °C and the pipe is closed at one end and open in the other, what is the frequency of the third harmonic (also called the 2nd overtone) for the pipe?\n\nPost your answer in hertz (Hz) and with 3 significant figures.\n<|assistant|>\nTo find the frequency of the third harmonic (the 2nd overtone) for the pipe, we need to first make sure the pipe is open at both ends and the air in the tube is still. Then we need to consider the frequency of the first harmonic, the fundamental frequency, as the starting point.\n\nThe fundamental frequency is determined by the length of the pipe and the density of the air. As the properties of air at 12.5 °C are given in the problem, we can find the speed of sound in air:\n\nCp = 1215.1 Pa·s·m^-1\n\nT = 12.5 degC = 125.05K = 125.05(73.14/100) ≈ 92.65 K\n\nv = sqrt(Cp/T) = sqrt(1215.1/92.65) ≈ 328.17 m/s\n\nThe length of the pipe is 37.5 cm and velocity of sound is 328.17 m/s.\n\nFor calculating frequency of third harmonic it is important to consider the size of the pipe, The formula for calculating fundamental frequency is:\n\n1/T = 1/L + 1/T1/2\n\nWhere T is the period of the fundamental frequency, T1/2 is the diameter of the wave, L is the length of the pipe, the frequency of the third harmonic will be 3* 1/T\n\nSo,\n\n1/T = 1/37.5 + 1/T1/2\n\n1/T = 1.7888 x 10^-12 s\n\nT = 1/1.7888 x 10^-12 s = 5551.5 Hz\n\nFrequency of the Third harmonic = 3 * 1/T = 3* 5551.5 = 16655 Hz\n\nSo the answer is 16655 Hz"}
