{"rejected": [{"content": "give me the commands to Real-Time Twitter Data Ingestion using kafka ubuntu", "role": "user"}, {"content": "Here is a step-by-step guide to help you set up real-time Twitter data ingestion using Kafka and Ubuntu:\n\n1. Install Apache Kafka:\n\n```\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install kafka\n```\n\n2. Start the Kafka service:\n\n```\nsudo systemctl start kafka\nsudo systemctl enable kafka\n```\n\n3. Create a topic for your data:\n\n```\nsudo kafka-topics.sh --create --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --replication-factor 1 --partitions 1 --topic twitter\n```\n\n4. Create a Zookeeper cluster:\n\nIf you do not already have a Zookeeper cluster set up, you can use the single-node setup by modifying the command below accordingly.\n\n```\nsudo kafka-server-start.sh --zookeeper localhost:2181 --topic twitter\n```\n\n5. Create a producer client:\n\n```\nkotlin\nimport kafka.api.Consumer\nimport kafka.config.ConsumerConfig\nimport kafka.consumer.{ConsumerParams, Subscription}\n\nobject TwitterConsumer {\n    def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = {\n        val config = ConsumerConfig(ConsumerParams.setup(\"localhost:9092\", \"twitter\"))\n        val consumer = new Consumer(config)\n        val subscription = Subscription(\"twitter\")\n        consumer.subscribe(listOf(subscription))\n        while (true) {\n            val message = consumer.poll(100).getOrNull\n            if (message == null) break\n            println(message.value())\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe code above will poll for 100 messages and print the message values. You can adjust this as per your requirements.\n\n6. Create a consumer client to read from the topic:\n\n```\nimport kafka.api.Consumer\nimport kafka.consumer.{ConsumerRecord, ConsumerParams, OffsetAndMetadata}\n\nobject TwitterConsumer {\n    def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = {\n        val config = ConsumerConfig(ConsumerParams.setup(\"localhost:9092\", \"twitter\"))\n        val consumer = new Consumer(config)\n        var totalOffsets: Option[Long] = None\n        while (true) {\n            val records = consumer.poll(100)\n            records.foreach { record =>\n                if (totalOffsets.isEmpty) {\n                    totalOffsets = Some(record.offset())\n                } else {\n                    totalOffsets = totalOffsets.map(_.plus(record.offset()))\n                }\n                //process record here\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n7. To consume the data in real-time, you can use a shell script or any other desired method that triggers the Kafka consumer periodically.\n\nI hope this helps you get started with real-time Twitter data ingestion using Kafka and Ubuntu!", "role": "assistant"}], "chosen_model": "Qwen/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct", "rejected_model": "mosaicml/mpt-7b-8k-chat", "id": "allenai/wildchat_v3.9_unused_off_policy_prompts-olmo32_32913", "source": "allenai/wildchat_v3.9_unused_off_policy_prompts-olmo32", "text": "<|user|>\ngive me the commands to Real-Time Twitter Data Ingestion using kafka ubuntu\n<|assistant|>\nHere is a step-by-step guide to help you set up real-time Twitter data ingestion using Kafka and Ubuntu:\n\n1. Install Apache Kafka:\n\n```\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install kafka\n```\n\n2. Start the Kafka service:\n\n```\nsudo systemctl start kafka\nsudo systemctl enable kafka\n```\n\n3. Create a topic for your data:\n\n```\nsudo kafka-topics.sh --create --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --replication-factor 1 --partitions 1 --topic twitter\n```\n\n4. Create a Zookeeper cluster:\n\nIf you do not already have a Zookeeper cluster set up, you can use the single-node setup by modifying the command below accordingly.\n\n```\nsudo kafka-server-start.sh --zookeeper localhost:2181 --topic twitter\n```\n\n5. Create a producer client:\n\n```\nkotlin\nimport kafka.api.Consumer\nimport kafka.config.ConsumerConfig\nimport kafka.consumer.{ConsumerParams, Subscription}\n\nobject TwitterConsumer {\n    def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = {\n        val config = ConsumerConfig(ConsumerParams.setup(\"localhost:9092\", \"twitter\"))\n        val consumer = new Consumer(config)\n        val subscription = Subscription(\"twitter\")\n        consumer.subscribe(listOf(subscription))\n        while (true) {\n            val message = consumer.poll(100).getOrNull\n            if (message == null) break\n            println(message.value())\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe code above will poll for 100 messages and print the message values. You can adjust this as per your requirements.\n\n6. Create a consumer client to read from the topic:\n\n```\nimport kafka.api.Consumer\nimport kafka.consumer.{ConsumerRecord, ConsumerParams, OffsetAndMetadata}\n\nobject TwitterConsumer {\n    def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = {\n        val config = ConsumerConfig(ConsumerParams.setup(\"localhost:9092\", \"twitter\"))\n        val consumer = new Consumer(config)\n        var totalOffsets: Option[Long] = None\n        while (true) {\n            val records = consumer.poll(100)\n            records.foreach { record =>\n                if (totalOffsets.isEmpty) {\n                    totalOffsets = Some(record.offset())\n                } else {\n                    totalOffsets = totalOffsets.map(_.plus(record.offset()))\n                }\n                //process record here\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n7. To consume the data in real-time, you can use a shell script or any other desired method that triggers the Kafka consumer periodically.\n\nI hope this helps you get started with real-time Twitter data ingestion using Kafka and Ubuntu!"}
